075 #edb/ref 17592186559508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559742 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559742 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559742 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559742 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bbbea0b5-e068-501a-85b9-14ebd116cadd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559769 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559769 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559769 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559769 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559769 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559769 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559769 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559769 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559769 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb3d5956-200b-5c5c-9bbb-4da333869f74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559774 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559774 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559774 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559774 #edb/ref 1071 "The irradiance $I,$ in $W/{m}^{2},$ of a lamp placed in the center of a spherical chamber varies inversely with the **square** of the radius $r$ of the spherical chamber, measured in meters. It is known that the irradiance is $0.6\\,W/{m}^{2}$ when the radius of the chamber is $3\\,m.$ What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $r?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559774 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04117d3f-552c-54f2-b117-fd9ff4546b57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559774 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the irradiance $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the radius of the spherical chamber, $r$, we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $I=0.6\\,W/{m}^{2}$ when $r=3\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.6 & =\\frac{k}{{3}^{2}} \\\\ 0.6 & =\\frac{k}{9} \\\\ 0.6\\cdot 9 & =k \\\\ 5.4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{5.4}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559783 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559783 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559783 #edb/ref 1071 "The intensity of radiation $I,$ in milliroentgen/hour ($\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$), spreading out from an X-ray device is inversely proportional to the **square** of the distance $d,$ measured in meters, between the device and the object being radiated. If a device that measures the radiation intensity registers $82\\,\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$ at a distance of $5\\,\\text{m}.$ What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $d?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ed2e1e9-66f4-55a8-9c62-03d8b636c4ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559783 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the intensity $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d,$ we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $82\\,\\text{mR}/\\text{h}$ when $d=5\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}82 & =\\frac{k}{{5}^{2}} \\\\ 82 & =\\frac{k}{25} \\\\ 82\\cdot 25 & =k \\\\ 2\\,050 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{2\\,050}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559835 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559835 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559835 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559835 #edb/ref 1071 "The time $t$, in minutes ($min$), it takes for an ice cream to melt is inversely proportional to the environment's temperature $T,$ in degrees Celsius (${}^{∘}C$). If it takes $10\\,min$ for an ice cream to melt at  ${30}^{∘}C,$ how much time it takes for the ice cream to melt at ${20}^{∘}C?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559835 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99714685-f61c-5dfd-b220-40d2fda79ac5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559835 #edb/ref 1074 "The time of melting $t$ is inversely proportional to the temperature $T,$ so we have\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{k}{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that it takes $10\\,min$ for an ice cream to melt at ${30}^{∘}C.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{k}{30} \\\\ 10\\cdot 30 & =k \\\\ 300 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{300}{T}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the time it takes for the ice cream to melt at ${20}^{∘}C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{300}{20}=15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time it takes for the ice cream to melt when $T={20}^{∘}C$ is $t=15\\,min.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559859 #edb/ref 1071 "For a fixed temperature, the pressure $P$ of a gas varies inversely with the volume $V$ that it occupies. When $V=3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ the pressure is $P=2$ megapascal ($\\text{MPa}$). What will be the pressure if the gas is compressed to a container with volume $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb89cc75-091f-5263-a465-e0004e0e6c85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559859 #edb/ref 1074 "The pressure $P$ is inversely proportional to the volume $V,$ so we have\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{k}{V},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the volume is $V=3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ the pressure is $P=2$ megapascal ($\\text{MPa}$). We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{3} \\\\ 2\\cdot 3 & =k \\\\ 6 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{6}{V}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the pressure when the volume is $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{6}{2}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the pressure of the gas when $V=2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}$ is $3\\,\\text{MPa}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559886 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559886 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559886 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559886 #edb/ref 1071 "The intensity $I$ of light, measured in $\\text{lux},$ is inversely proportional to the **square** of the distance $d,$ measured in meters, between the light source and the object being illuminated. A device that measures the light intensity is $12$ meters away from the light source and registers $4$ $\\text{lux}.$  What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $d?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559886 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5ab26d2-91d7-599f-b642-d61eec8ddc94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559886 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the intensity $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d,$ we must have\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $I=4\\,\\text{lux}$ when $d=12\\,\\text{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =\\frac{k}{{12}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{k}{144} \\\\ 4\\cdot 144 & =k \\\\ 576 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{576}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559911 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c17c573-86f7-52cc-9a73-40fbc246029e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559911 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559916 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559916 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3811cab8-bfed-5a2b-a00d-bd53f4bc212a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559916 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559931 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55e23826-9116-5685-8d98-1930337eae6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559936 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559936 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6195b08a-b671-5086-8dde-dbc84452e8ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559936 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559957 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559957 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac02dbdc-cd9f-55e1-8008-da2bd4772a77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559957 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559859 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
